\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $n\geq1$ and $f(z)=\sum_{j=0}^n a_jz^j$ with all $a_j>0$, and put $a_{-1}=a_{n+1}=0$. For an increasing index list $0=k_1<\cdots<k_m=n$, define its parity-change count by
\[v=\bigl|\{j:1\leq j<m,\ k_j\not\equiv k_{j+1}\pmod2\}\bigr|.\]
Real roots are counted with multiplicity.\par Put $c_j=a_j^2-a_{j-1}a_{j+1}$, and let $k_1,\ldots,k_m$ be exactly the indices for which $c_j\geq0$, in increasing order. Both endpoint indices belong to this list. Write $v_{10}(f)$ for its parity-change count.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is the number of real zeros of $f$ at most $v_{10}(f)$ for every such $f$? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.05295}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does the parity-change count among indices with nonnegative ordinary coefficient differences bound the number of real roots?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 2200015 (AMR-021-0015). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Boris Shapiro, Problems Around Polynomials -- The Good, The Bad and The Ugly, Arnold Mathematical Journal 1 (2015), pp.91--104, Conjecture 10 and its complete preceding definitions. Original question and full discussion. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.05295}.

